WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.897
Task statement 3.2.

2
00:00:01.897 --> 00:00:05.869
Create and configure custom slash commands and skills.

3
00:00:05.869 --> 00:00:13.812
Your team types the same 20-line PR review prompt every day; a shared skill turns it into one slash command.

4
00:00:15.212 --> 00:00:20.433
A custom command is a prompt you saved so you can run it with a slash and a name.

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:26.428
A skill goes a step further: a folder with a SKILL dot md file and any helper files it needs.

6
00:00:26.428 --> 00:00:29.522
The key difference from CLAUDE dot md is timing.

7
00:00:29.522 --> 00:00:36.097
CLAUDE dot md is always loaded, while a skill loads only when someone calls it or it becomes relevant.

8
00:00:37.497 --> 00:00:39.323
Location decides who gets it.

9
00:00:39.323 --> 00:00:45.306
Anything inside the repository's dot claude folder is committed and every teammate receives it.

10
00:00:45.306 --> 00:00:48.076
Anything in your home folder is yours alone.

11
00:00:48.076 --> 00:00:54.625
Inside the prompt, the arguments placeholder is replaced with whatever was typed after the command name.

12
00:00:54.625 --> 00:00:59.663
Current docs treat commands and skills as one system, and both forms still work.

13
00:01:01.063 --> 00:01:03.931
Three frontmatter fields show up on the exam.

14
00:01:03.931 --> 00:01:11.325
Context fork runs the skill in a separate subagent, so a long, noisy analysis does not flood your main conversation.

15
00:01:11.325 --> 00:01:14.576
Allowed tools names the tools the skill works with.

16
00:01:14.576 --> 00:01:17.253
Argument hint shows the user what to type.

17
00:01:17.253 --> 00:01:22.225
In current docs, allowed tools pre-approves tools rather than blocking others.

18
00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:25.722
Picture the code generation team.

19
00:01:25.722 --> 00:01:31.061
They want one shared review workflow, so it goes in the repository and is committed.

20
00:01:31.061 --> 00:01:34.429
Someone's personal helper stays in their home folder.

21
00:01:34.429 --> 00:01:39.894
A skill that scans hundreds of files gets context fork so only its summary comes back.

22
00:01:39.894 --> 00:01:44.724
And the style rules everyone must follow all the time stay in CLAUDE dot md.

23
00:01:46.124 --> 00:01:48.292
The exam likes three confusions.

24
00:01:48.292 --> 00:01:50.460
Personal versus shared location.

25
00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.577
Inline versus forked execution for noisy work.

26
00:01:53.577 --> 00:01:56.220
And on-demand versus always-on content.

27
00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:00.692
When a question says the whole team, look for the repository path.

28
00:02:00.692 --> 00:02:05.367
When it says output is cluttering the session, look for context fork.

29
00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:08.343
Keep four things in mind.

30
00:02:08.343 --> 00:02:10.929
Where the file lives decides who gets it.

31
00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:14.398
Context fork keeps noisy work out of your main session.

32
00:02:14.398 --> 00:02:19.317
Allowed tools and argument hint shape how a skill runs and how people call it.

33
00:02:19.317 --> 00:02:25.497
And use skills for workflows you call on demand, while CLAUDE dot md holds what must always apply.

